  silently stale.
misalignment:
  - plan-gap
  - spec-gap
---

## Issues

- [ ] **README.md:25** — Quick Start block uses `npx @aidemd-mcp/server init` without `@latest`. The spec requires `@latest` on all npx invocations ("use `npx -y @aidemd-mcp/server@latest` rather than a bare package name. The bare name uses a locally cached version if one exists"). The plan carried this omission forward (plan.aide line 34 shows `npx @aidemd-mcp/server init` without `@latest`), meaning the gap originated at planning, not implementation.
      Traces to: `.aide/readme.aide` outcomes.undesired[4] ("A config block using a bare package name without @latest, which silently serves a stale cached version to users who have previously installed any version") | Misalignment: `plan-gap`

- [ ] **`.aide/readme.aide` Context lines 81-83** — The Context paragraph hardcodes a tool count and enumerates the canonical roster: "The server currently ships six tools (`aide_discover`, `aide_read`, `aide_scaffold`, `aide_validate`, `aide_init`, `aide_upgrade`) plus a `plan.aide` file type. With six tools, full parameter documentation is feasible..." The actual surface is now eight tools — the six listed plus `aide_info` and `aide_inspect` (both registered in `src/index.ts` and surfaced through the handshake). The Context paragraph's reasoning ("six tools is small enough that every parameter can be documented... ten or fewer tools") cascades into the Strategy decision to "Document every tool with full parameters" — a decision that may or may not still hold at eight tools, but the spec gives the reader no way to know because the count is silently stale.
      Traces to:
        - `src/.aide` outcomes.undesired[6] ("A spec, test, or description that pins a specific count of tools or enumerates the tool roster as part of the contract. The count is an implementation detail of the current methodology snapshot, not an invariant — encoding it guarantees drift the moment a new lifecycle phase is added.")
        - `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.undesired[3] ("A canonical doc that describes behavior the server does not actually deliver. Aspirational docs are worse than missing docs, because missing docs do not lie.") — the README spec's "six tools" enumeration is the reverse failure mode: the doc describes an old surface the server has since outgrown, and a downstream README written from this spec will under-document the actual tooling.
      Misalignment: `spec-gap`

- [ ] **`.aide/readme.aide` outcomes.desired[1]** — The desired outcome says "The tools section documents every tool with full parameter lists in the canonical MCP README format... so a developer evaluating the server knows exactly what capabilities they get before installing." This outcome inherits the Context's six-tool enumeration by reference: "every tool" is bounded by whatever the Context counts, and the Context counts six. Any README produced from this spec will document six tools and silently omit `aide_info` and `aide_inspect`, leaving evaluating developers unaware of two real capabilities. The drift is silent because the outcome reads as exhaustive ("every tool") while the spec internally pins the meaning of "every" to a stale count.
      Traces to:
        - `src/.aide` outcomes.desired[1] ("Every tool description is complete enough to teach the convention it invokes on its own. An agent reading only the tool list learns what `.aide` files are...") — a README that documents six of eight tools fails this principle for the two omitted tools.
        - `src/.aide` outcomes.undesired[6] (same count-pinning rule as above)
      Misalignment: `spec-gap`

## Retro

The first issue (Quick Start `@latest`) is plan-level: the planner read the
`@latest` requirement as scoped only to JSON config blocks and did not apply
it to the `npx @aidemd-mcp/server init` CLI command. That retro is preserved
unchanged from the prior QA pass.

The second and third issues are spec-level alignment drift that should have
been caught when `aide_info` and `aide_inspect` were added to the server. The
exact same drift pattern is already flagged at `src/todo.aide` against
`src/.aide` outcomes.desired[0] — the count and roster enumeration drift hit
two specs simultaneously, but the realignment fix landed in neither. The
README spec's Context paragraph and outcomes.desired[1] share the same blast
radius: any new tool added to the surface silently invalidates both the
count and the implicit "every tool" scope without any signal to the spec
author.

What would have caught this earlier:
- An aide-aligner pass after every `src/index.ts` registration change. The
  prior QA log on `src/.aide` already names this gap ("Any commit that adds
  a new `case` to the `CallToolRequestSchema` handler in `src/index.ts`
  should trigger a review of `src/.aide` outcomes.desired[0]") — the
  alignment cascade up to `.aide/readme.aide` was missed because no agent
  walked beyond the immediate parent.
- A spec-writer rule against numeric tool counts and roster enumerations
  in any spec under the project. The root `.aide/intent.aide` and parent
  `src/.aide` both forbid these encodings; the README spec inherited the
  rule but encoded the exact pattern the rule warns against. The aligner
  needs to catch these inheritance violations on every alignment pass.
